              "explanation": "#### Extended verification and failure-mode analysis\n\nCancellation and completion may race. A submitted asynchronous read can remain active after the requesting task decides it no longer needs the result. If the buffer is released while the device or kernel may still write into it, memory corruption can occur. The program must follow the relevant API's explicit cancellation guarantee, manage buffer ownership until completion or a confirmed quiescent state, and ensure only one final outcome is published. One common logical pattern tracks a generation or operation identifier so that a late result can be ignored without invalidating its storage prematurely. Test four event orders: completion before cancellation, cancellation before completion, cancellation request with confirmed cancellation, and operation failure concurrent with timeout. In each case, verify one cleanup path and no use-after-free. This reasoning is about lifecycle correctness, not a guarantee that every OS exposes the same cancellation primitive."
